findings.body:
**`sshQueueTransport` ignores the caller lifetime. A request can run past the caller deadline or abort.**

The transport is `(request) => ...`. It drops the `options` argument that `request()` passes through `requestLifetime(options)`. As a result:
- An abort of `options.signal` does not kill the `ssh` child.
- The fixed 45000 ms timer at Line 124 starts fresh for every page. It is not clamped to `remainingMs()`.

`refuseOnLifetime` only checks the budget before a request starts. The protocol comment at `queueProtocol.ts:54` says the budget is "never renewed per page". With this transport, one in-flight request can overshoot the caller deadline by up to 45 s. A cancelled `report` also keeps a live `ssh` process until it finishes or times out.

Honor `options.signal` and clamp the timer to the remaining budget.

<details>
<summary>Proposed fix</summary>

```diff
-  return (request) =>
+  return (request, options) =>
     new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
+      options?.signal?.throwIfAborted();
       if (Buffer.byteLength(request) > QUEUE_WIRE_BYTES) {
@@
-      const timer = setTimeout(() => {
+      const remaining = options?.remainingMs?.();
+      const budget = remaining === undefined || !Number.isFinite(remaining) ? 45000 : Math.max(0, remaining);
+      const timer = setTimeout(() => {
         child.kill('SIGKILL');
         reject(new Error('queue delivery unknown: timeout'));
-      }, 45000);
+      }, Math.min(45000, budget));
+      options?.signal?.addEventListener(
+        'abort',
+        () => {
+          child.kill('SIGKILL');
+          reject(options.signal?.reason);
+        },
+        { once: true },
+      );
```
</details>

Add a test that aborts mid-request and asserts the child is killed.






Also applies to: 124-127
